{"date": "2026-11-16", "post_id": 1511616, "title": "NGC 7129: Star Formation with Jets from Webb", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-16-ngc-7129-star-formation-with-jets-from-webb/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> How do stars form? To help answer this question, <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> used the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/\">James Webb Space Telescope</a> to take the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/ngc-7129-nircam-image/\">featured detailed image</a> of the star-forming region <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NGC_7129\">NGC 7129</a> in <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/07_infraredwaves/\">infrared light</a>. Star formation progresses when <a href=\"https://www.labxchange.org/library/items/lb:LabXchange:bc30bab5:lx_simulation:1\">gravity</a> pulls together massive amounts of gas, but soon things can get complicated when previously formed stars emit powerful <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-july-28-young-stars-stellar-jets/\">jets</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-february-3-wolf-rayet-star-124-stellar-wind-machine/\">winds</a> -- and some then <a href=\"https://youtu.be/-yEBVm5o97E\">explode</a>. Opaque <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/dust-in-the-stellar-wind-a-cosmological-primer/\">dust</a> can form in the cool <a href=\"https://www.eso.org/sci/meetings/2024/Bthelines/Ludwig.pdf\">atmospheres of stars</a> and, when dispersed, can obstruct <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/09_visiblelight/\">visible-light</a> images. Many stars can be seen to be forming in the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/ngc-7129-nircam-image/#:~:text=Interactive:\">featured image</a>, with the young stars on the left, in the gold-colored gas, typically being more mature than the young stars on the right -- in the red region. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-may-28-herbig-haro-24/\">Jets</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2000-march-18-a-wind-from-the-sun/\">wind</a>s likely cause the filamentary nature of the red-colored gas. Studying stars at different stages of development <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1734167121479-6147220c23f0?q=80&amp;w=687&amp;auto=format&amp;fit=crop&amp;ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;ixid=NHwxMjA3fDA%3D\">bolsters understanding</a> about what <a href=\"https://scixplorer.org/abs/2020SSRv..216...68G/abstract\">physical processes</a> are most important and when.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> sky duel", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/\">ESA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.asc-csa.gc.ca/eng/\">CSA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.stsci.edu/\">STScI</a>, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/\">JWST</a>; <br>Processing: <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/alyssa-pagan-058170144\">A. Pagan</a> (<a href=\"https://www.stsci.edu/\">STScI</a>)", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/\">ESA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.asc-csa.gc.ca/eng/\">CSA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.stsci.edu/\">STScI</a>, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/\">JWST</a>; <br>Processing: <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/alyssa-pagan-058170144\">A. Pagan</a> (<a href=\"https://www.stsci.edu/\">STScI</a>)", "alt": "A young star cluster filled with many stars that display Webb\u2019s unique eight-pronged diffraction pattern. At its center is a yellow star that sports the largest diffraction pattern. To its left, there is a region filled with yellow dust and gas that extends from the star to the left and the bottom, covering about two-thirds of the frame. The yellow region has several embedded blue stars of different sizes. The top third, which lies outside the yellow region, has a few bright protostars, within dense gray gas. These stars illuminate the gas, making it appear blue. A few red outflows from other protostars are visible above this grayish-blue region. To the right of the central star, there is a clumpy, flame-like plume of red dust and gas with protostars that emit a soft white light within. The plume is surrounded by more dense gray gas and dust. It is about one third the size of the yellow region. There are several background galaxies strewn throughout.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-16-ngc-7129-star-formation-with-jets-from-webb/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/missions/webb/science/2026/10/weic2620a.jpg?w=1280&h=1194&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 16 - NGC 7129: Star Formation with Jets from Webb</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-16-ngc-7129-star-formation-with-jets-from-webb/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: How do stars form? To help answer this question, NASA used the James Webb Space Telescope to take the featured detailed image of the star-forming region NGC 7129 in infrared light. Star formation progresses when gravity pulls together massive amounts of gas, but soon things can get complicated when previously formed stars emit powerful jets and winds -- and some then explode. Opaque dust can form in the cool atmospheres of stars and, when dispersed, can obstruct visible-light images. Many stars can be seen to be forming in the featured image, with the young stars on the left, in the gold-colored gas, typically being more mature than the young stars on the right -- in the red region. Jets and winds likely cause the filamentary nature of the red-colored gas. Studying stars at different stages of development bolsters understanding about what physical processes are most important and when.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: sky duel\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"ngc 7129: star formation with jets from webb\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"NGC 7129: Star Formation with Jets from Webb\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-16-ngc-7129-star-formation-with-jets-from-webb/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/missions/webb/science/2026/10/weic2620a.jpg?w=1280&#038;h=1194&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/missions/webb/science/2026/10/weic2620a.jpg?w=1280&#038;h=1194&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A young star cluster filled with many stars that display Webb\u2019s unique eight-pronged diffraction pattern. At its center is a yellow star that sports the largest diffraction pattern. To its left, there is a region filled with yellow dust and gas that extends from the star to the left and the bottom, covering about two-thirds of the frame. The yellow region has several embedded blue stars of different sizes. The top third, which lies outside the yellow region, has a few bright protostars, within dense gray gas. These stars illuminate the gas, making it appear blue. A few red outflows from other protostars are visible above this grayish-blue region. To the right of the central star, there is a clumpy, flame-like plume of red dust and gas with protostars that emit a soft white light within. The plume is surrounded by more dense gray gas and dust. It is about one third the size of the yellow region. There are several background galaxies strewn throughout.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-16T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-15T20:23:37-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"NGC 7129: Star Formation with Jets from Webb\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/missions/webb/science/2026/10/weic2620a.jpg?w=1280&#038;h=1194&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 16<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/missions/webb/science/2026/10/weic2620a.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/missions/webb/science/2026/10/weic2620a.jpg\" alt=\"A young star cluster filled with many stars that display Webb\u2019s unique eight-pronged diffraction pattern. At its center is a yellow star that sports the largest diffraction pattern. To its left, there is a region filled with yellow dust and gas that extends from the star to the left and the bottom, covering about two-thirds of the frame. The yellow region has several embedded blue stars of different sizes. The top third, which lies outside the yellow region, has a few bright protostars, within dense gray gas. These stars illuminate the gas, making it appear blue. A few red outflows from other protostars are visible above this grayish-blue region. To the right of the central star, there is a clumpy, flame-like plume of red dust and gas with protostars that emit a soft white light within. The plume is surrounded by more dense gray gas and dust. It is about one third the size of the yellow region. There are several background galaxies strewn throughout.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> NGC 7129: Star Formation with Jets from Webb </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/\">ESA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.asc-csa.gc.ca/eng/\">CSA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.stsci.edu/\">STScI</a>, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/\">JWST</a>; <br>Processing: <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/alyssa-pagan-058170144\">A. Pagan</a> (<a href=\"https://www.stsci.edu/\">STScI</a>)\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> How do stars form? To help answer this question, <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> used the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/\">James Webb Space Telescope</a> to take the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/ngc-7129-nircam-image/\">featured detailed image</a> of the star-forming region <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NGC_7129\">NGC 7129</a> in <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/07_infraredwaves/\">infrared light</a>. Star formation progresses when <a href=\"https://www.labxchange.org/library/items/lb:LabXchange:bc30bab5:lx_simulation:1\">gravity</a> pulls together massive amounts of gas, but soon things can get complicated when previously formed stars emit powerful <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-july-28-young-stars-stellar-jets/\">jets</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-february-3-wolf-rayet-star-124-stellar-wind-machine/\">winds</a> -- and some then <a href=\"https://youtu.be/-yEBVm5o97E\">explode</a>. Opaque <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/dust-in-the-stellar-wind-a-cosmological-primer/\">dust</a> can form in the cool <a href=\"https://www.eso.org/sci/meetings/2024/Bthelines/Ludwig.pdf\">atmospheres of stars</a> and, when dispersed, can obstruct <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/09_visiblelight/\">visible-light</a> images. Many stars can be seen to be forming in the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/ngc-7129-nircam-image/#:~:text=Interactive:\">featured image</a>, with the young stars on the left, in the gold-colored gas, typically being more mature than the young stars on the right -- in the red region. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-may-28-herbig-haro-24/\">Jets</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2000-march-18-a-wind-from-the-sun/\">wind</a>s likely cause the filamentary nature of the red-colored gas. Studying stars at different stages of development <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1734167121479-6147220c23f0?q=80&amp;w=687&amp;auto=format&amp;fit=crop&amp;ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;ixid=NHwxMjA3fDA%3D\">bolsters understanding</a> about what <a href=\"https://scixplorer.org/abs/2020SSRv..216...68G/abstract\">physical processes</a> are most important and when.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> sky duel\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-15-leonid-meteors-through-orion/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261116/html"}